and we believe that all software should be reliable and observable and scalable by default.

36
00:02:28,229 --> 00:02:32,869
So now deboss stands for durable backends observable and scalable.

37
00:02:32,869 --> 00:02:37,910
So you mentioned the use of databases for basically running your applications.

38
00:02:37,910 --> 00:02:40,070
but why is that a new concept?

39
00:02:40,070 --> 00:02:44,790
Everybody uses databases for running apps or most people do.

40
00:02:44,790 --> 00:02:48,949
So what is the difference here? What is the secret sauce that you talking about?

41
00:02:48,959 --> 00:02:51,750
Yeah.

42
00:02:51,750 --> 00:02:57,911
So it's true that people have been storing their business critical data in databases for like 30 40. years.

43
00:02:57,911 --> 00:03:07,589
but the new concept is that we also want to persist programs execution state in the database.

44
00:03:07,589 --> 00:03:12,869
like here's your program and it has multiple steps and we want to persist the steps output.

45
00:03:12,869 --> 00:03:15,190
and the input into the database so.

46
00:03:15,190 --> 00:03:19,750
that if your program crashes or machine failed we'll be able to resume from exactly.

47
00:03:19,750 --> 00:03:22,869
where you left off.

48
00:03:22,869 --> 00:03:25,350
So the idea is to in addition to your application data,

49
00:03:25,350 --> 00:03:30,149
we also store your program execution state in the database.

50
00:03:30,149 --> 00:03:34,949
Especially if you're having long running and dynamic workflows in your programs,

51
00:03:34,949 --> 00:03:39,750
you really don't want to restart from scratch every time you hit a system error.

52
00:03:39,750 --> 00:03:42,070
or you have a machine failure.

53
00:03:42,070 --> 00:03:47,270
So Chan, maybe you can explain to the listeners what exactly do you define a workflow as opposed.

54
00:03:47,270 --> 00:03:50,230
to a service? Yeah.

55
00:03:50,230 --> 00:03:55,110
So to get started I think we can talk about what is a workflow in this context.

56
00:03:55,110 --> 00:03:57,750
So traditionally people think workflows as state machines.

57
00:03:57,750 --> 00:03:59,910
You have to define a DAC stuff like that.

58
00:03:59,920 --> 00:04:04,869
But actually in deboss anything can be a workflow.

59
00:04:04,869 --> 00:04:12,309
So to give a concrete example a workflow is a sequence of operations or function calls.

60
00:04:12,309 --> 00:04:18,069
So a very typical example in like durable execution is let's say checkout service.

61
00:04:18,069 --> 00:04:24,870
So if you're implementing a checkout service usually you have to call say reserve inventory you have to.

62
00:04:24,870 --> 00:04:27,030
update a database to make sure that you have enough inventory.

63
00:04:27,030 --> 00:04:35,189
and then after that if you successfully reserve the inventory you will cut out to the payment process.

64
00:04:35,189 --> 00:04:37,350
for example this could be an external service like stripe.

65
00:04:37,360 --> 00:04:43,110
or PayPal you will say I want to charge a user this much.

66
00:04:43,110 --> 00:04:47,510
and after that you need to wait for the response from those services.

67
00:04:47,510 --> 00:04:51,350
and then based on the result you will decide whether to fulfill the order.

68
00:04:51,350 --> 00:04:54,950
and send a confirmation email to the user.

69
00:04:54,950 --> 00:04:57,110
or you have to undo your reservation for the inventory.

70
00:04:57,110 --> 00:05:02,550
and then cancel the order and send a cancellation email.

71
00:05:02,550 --> 00:05:05,189
So this process can be abstracted as a workflow.

72
00:05:05,189 --> 00:05:07,029
and then like what guarantees do we want for this workflow?

73
00:05:07,039 --> 00:05:11,909
First we want to make sure that once I click.

74
00:05:11,909 --> 00:05:15,510
that check out button all steps will eventually succeed or complete.

75
00:05:15,520 --> 00:05:18,150
Right?

76
00:05:18,150 --> 00:05:20,950
I don't want to say I pay for my process.

77
00:05:20,950 --> 00:05:24,790
but I never receive my item or I reserve the inventory but never charge the user.

78
00:05:24,790 --> 00:05:30,390
So that's the first guarantee and the second guarantee is that I want to guarantee effectively exactly once.

79
00:05:30,400 --> 00:05:34,950
So if I charge a user I want to charge them once.

80
00:05:34,950 --> 00:05:38,230
and if I reserve the inventory I also want to only reserve it once.

81
00:05:38,240 --> 00:05:42,230
So you talked about workflow and you mentioned transaction.

82
00:05:42,230 --> 00:05:45,189
What's the relationship?

83
00:05:45,189 --> 00:05:51,510
So in this context a transaction is essentially interaction to a local database.

84
00:05:51,510 --> 00:05:55,110
and then a workflow can be composed of transactions.

85
00:05:55,110 --> 00:05:59,749
and also external services or we just call it steps in general.

86
00:05:59,749 --> 00:06:03,029
So in deboss a workflow is a sequence of steps.

87
00:06:03,029 --> 00:06:08,870
and some steps could be transactions that talk to a database.

88
00:06:08,870 --> 00:06:14,710
and other normal steps well normal steps can be any functions right can be talking to external APIs.

89
00:06:14,710 --> 00:06:20,790
or can have some any non-deterministic factors operations in.

90
00:06:20,790 --> 00:06:22,870
that step but overall the workflow needs to be deterministic.

91
00:06:22,870 --> 00:06:27,990
and determinism and item potency are two foundations for durable workflows.

92
00:06:27,990 --> 00:06:33,510
and essentially What we build is a library that you can just easily install it.

93
00:06:33,510 --> 00:06:37,670
and then add annotations to your program.

94
00:06:37,670 --> 00:06:42,070
So I described the checkout workflow to make it durable in deboss.

95
00:06:42,070 --> 00:06:49,430
You essentially put annotation at deboss.workflow in front of the overall orchestration workflow function.

96
00:06:49,430 --> 00:06:55,029
and then you put at step at the function definition of every individual step.

97
00:06:55,039 --> 00:07:00,309
Then when we call that function essentially we'll first we.

98
00:07:00,309 --> 00:07:04,309
basically put wrappers around those functions to make them durable.

99
00:07:04,309 --> 00:07:06,469
So to make it more concrete basically.

100
00:07:06,469 --> 00:07:14,070
when you start a workflow when you start call the workflow function the wrapper would first checkpoint the.

101
00:07:14,070 --> 00:07:17,351
input of the workflow. in the database.

102
00:07:17,351 --> 00:07:23,189
and then for each step we first check have we executed this step before.

103
00:07:23,189 --> 00:07:27,909
If so, we directly read the recorded output from the database.

104
00:07:27,909 --> 00:07:30,390
and return the output instead of re-executing it.

105
00:07:30,390 --> 00:07:33,909
So this way we guarantee exactly once.

106
00:07:33,909 --> 00:07:41,111
So the thing is for external systems the way to guarantee exactly once is to guarantee at least. once plus item potency key.

107
00:07:41,111 --> 00:07:49,510
and deboss basically automatically generates an item potency key per workflow and per step.

108
00:07:49,510 --> 00:07:52,309
So we can guarantee that each step will execute effectively exactly once.

109
00:07:52,319 --> 00:07:56,950
and then after each step finishes we'll checkpoint the output into the database.

110
00:07:56,960 --> 00:08:02,150
and so on so forth until we reach the end of the workflow.

111
00:08:02,150 --> 00:08:04,869
and say if something crashes in the middle.

112
00:08:04,869 --> 00:08:11,270
when we recover the deboss library will look into the database and see which workflow is still pending.

113
00:08:11,270 --> 00:08:13,189
And then if the workflow is still pending,

114
00:08:13,189 --> 00:08:18,390
we'll just essentially replay the workflow from the start and walk through all the steps.

115
00:08:18,390 --> 00:08:23,189
And then for the finished steps, we'll already see the database record.

116
00:08:23,189 --> 00:08:25,670
And then we'll skip those steps.

117
00:08:25,670 --> 00:08:28,869
And then we'll eventually lend to the last completed step.

118
00:08:28,869 --> 00:08:33,909
and then we'll continue resume the workflow from where it left off.

119
00:08:33,909 --> 00:08:36,469
So that's basically a highle idea of how deboss.

120
00:08:36,479 --> 00:08:38,870
You mentioned the item potency key.

121
00:08:38,880 --> 00:08:40,389
explain that further.

122
00:08:40,399 --> 00:08:44,310
Yeah.

123
00:08:44,310 --> 00:08:47,110
So essentially item potency key is a way to guarantee.

124
00:08:47,110 --> 00:08:50,710
that each step is executed once and only once.

125
00:08:50,710 --> 00:08:55,670
So essentially when you work with external API say stripe payment.

126
00:08:55,670 --> 00:09:01,190
right you want to say I only want to charge it this user once.

127
00:09:01,190 --> 00:09:05,990
and then you can pick it back a item potency key in your request to stripe.

128
00:09:05,990 --> 00:09:08,550
So if Stripe received the same key again,

129
00:09:08,550 --> 00:09:11,190
it will not issue another invoice to the user,

130
00:09:11,190 --> 00:09:13,430
Stripe will be able to look up its own database.

131
00:09:13,430 --> 00:09:17,829
and say, "Hey, I already have an outstanding invoice for this checkout session.".

132
00:09:17,829 --> 00:09:20,230
So they will only charge a user once.

133
00:09:20,230 --> 00:09:25,430
So I think item potency is a very important concept in terms of durable execution.

134
00:09:25,440 --> 00:09:29,990
And in Deboss, we automatically generate an item potency key for a workflow.

135
00:09:29,990 --> 00:09:31,990
if you don't already provide one.

136
00:09:31,990 --> 00:09:35,910
And then for each step we'll basically append the step sequence number to it.

137
00:09:35,910 --> 00:09:40,949
So every step in the workflow will also be uniquely identified.

138
00:09:40,949 --> 00:09:44,389
So the reason we need item potency key is.

139
00:09:44,389 --> 00:09:52,070
that you can't really control external systems right say even if we only call stripe API once.

140
00:09:52,070 --> 00:09:54,150
We don't know whether the network connection will fail.

141
00:09:54,150 --> 00:09:59,269
or maybe stripe will fail or there's some intermediate transient errors that may happen.

142
00:09:59,269 --> 00:10:05,269
So like if we don't get the result back, we may think it failed.

143
00:10:05,269 --> 00:10:09,030
but in order to make sure that the checkout goes through,

144
00:10:09,030 --> 00:10:11,030
we may want to retry it and we retry it.

145
00:10:11,030 --> 00:10:15,269
We can't generate a new session because maybe the stripe payment already succeeded,

146
00:10:15,269 --> 00:10:18,949
but it just the network was cut when Stripe tried to send the result back.

147
00:10:18,949 --> 00:10:21,269
So we have to retry it.

148
00:10:21,269 --> 00:10:23,590
And when we retry it, the way to tell Stripe.

149
00:10:23,590 --> 00:10:28,470
that this is the same checkout request I sent it before is to append the item potency key.

150
00:10:28,470 --> 00:10:33,590
So item policy key is the key to guarantee correctness.

151
00:10:33,590 --> 00:10:35,910
and exactly once execution when you work with external systems.

152
00:10:35,920 --> 00:10:40,389
Could you talk more about the deboss architecture?

153
00:10:40,389 --> 00:10:42,790
You did talk about the workflow and the step.

154
00:10:42,790 --> 00:10:47,190
Is there any other key elements of the architecture you'd like people to know about.

155
00:10:47,190 --> 00:10:51,990
and especially if they have origins in the research that you had done in the past?

156
00:10:52,000 --> 00:10:54,870
Yeah.

157
00:10:54,870 --> 00:11:00,150
So deboss workflows and steps and transactions were implemented directly as SQL interactions in the library.

158
00:11:00,150 --> 00:11:06,069
And then beyond that we also have several other primitives.

159
00:11:06,069 --> 00:11:08,550
The one is widely used is called deboss cues.

160
00:11:08,550 --> 00:11:11,829
So we also implement cues on top of postgress.

161
00:11:11,829 --> 00:11:16,949
With cues basically you will be able to group.

162
00:11:16,949 --> 00:11:19,670
So instead of directly invoking or executing a workflow synchronously,

163
00:11:19,680 --> 00:11:25,190
you can incue a workflow and then other workers will be able to pick it up.

164
00:11:25,190 --> 00:11:31,509
So the Q is also just a database record, right?

165
00:11:31,509 --> 00:11:35,350
We just inq by saying here's the task,

166
00:11:35,350 --> 00:11:37,670
here's a Q name and then for the worker they can just pull from each Q.

167
00:11:37,680 --> 00:11:45,750
So the benefit of Q is that it makes it really easy to control concurrency and rate limiting.

168
00:11:45,750 --> 00:11:50,710
Say for example we can have different cues like open AIQ, cloud Q, stuff like that.

169
00:11:50,710 --> 00:11:56,711
and we want to say for the open AIQ we only want to send the request five times. per minute.

170
00:11:56,711 --> 00:11:59,590
So this is a way to group your invocations.

171
00:11:59,600 --> 00:12:04,389
and to control how many outstanding requests you send to external systems.

172
00:12:04,399 --> 00:12:09,670
And another thing is based on research is messaging system.

173
00:12:09,670 --> 00:12:14,230
So when workflows and steps basically they are all within the deboss territory.

174
00:12:14,230 --> 00:12:22,389
but when you need to interact with external systems you need a way to communicate with the workflow.

175
00:12:22,389 --> 00:12:24,710
So give an example of this so say actually the checkout workflow right.

176
00:12:24,720 --> 00:12:31,030
when you call stripe you don't block and wait until user to pay out.

177
00:12:31,030 --> 00:12:35,430
So what you actually do is that Stripe will directly return a confirmation saying.

178
00:12:35,430 --> 00:12:37,509
okay we are processing your payment.

179
00:12:37,509 --> 00:12:42,790
but when your payment is done we'll send you a web hook invocation to say this payment is.

180
00:12:42,800 --> 00:12:46,070
done. and now how to do it in deboss is.

181
00:12:46,079 --> 00:12:54,390
that so in a workflow we can have a deboss receive on a specific topic waiting for the. specific stripe payment.

182
00:12:54,399 --> 00:12:57,110
session.

183
00:12:57,110 --> 00:13:00,230
and then we can implement a web hook.

184
00:13:00,230 --> 00:13:02,629
that listens Stripe call back when a stripe call back we can extract.

185
00:13:02,639 --> 00:13:06,629
because as we mentioned we have the item policy key we can uniquely identify.

186
00:13:06,639 --> 00:13:08,470
which workflow we should call back to.

187
00:13:08,480 --> 00:13:15,110
and then we will be able to send say deboss send with.

188
00:13:15,110 --> 00:13:22,710
that item potency key and then send stripe payments result either paid or payment rejected stuff like that.

189
00:13:22,710 --> 00:13:24,389
and then the workflow well using the receive will be able to get.

190
00:13:24,399 --> 00:13:33,269
that information so this is really useful if you want to like I think almost all.

191
00:13:33,269 --> 00:13:35,750
the payment system for example is using this type of pattern.

192
00:13:35,760 --> 00:13:43,111
and also if you want anything that goes to like human in the loop say you want to. send a human verification email.

193
00:13:43,111 --> 00:13:50,870
and then you want to wait for the human confirmation to come back you can't just block there.

194
00:13:50,870 --> 00:13:54,230
and wait you have to wait for a call back.

195
00:13:54,230 --> 00:13:57,430
and then deboss send and receive primitive allows you to do that.

196
00:13:57,440 --> 00:14:03,189
and this library is what you call as deboss transact.

197
00:14:03,189 --> 00:14:05,991
Anybody can use it as long as they annotate their code. Yes.

198
00:14:06,000 --> 00:14:11,031
So deboss transact is an open source library. It's MIT license.

199
00:14:11,031 --> 00:14:13,110
So anyone will be able to use it.

200
00:14:13,110 --> 00:14:16,150
It's currently available in Typescript and Python.

201
00:14:16,150 --> 00:14:18,790
So we are adding more language support.

202
00:14:18,790 --> 00:14:24,629
So for the audience if you have any feedback on what languages you wish to see,

203
00:14:24,629 --> 00:14:27,110
please contact us and we'll add more.

204
00:14:27,110 --> 00:14:29,430
So for the transact library you can install it and run it anywhere.

205
00:14:29,440 --> 00:14:31,430
What is the deboss cloud?

206
00:14:31,440 --> 00:14:34,389
Right.

207
00:14:34,389 --> 00:14:39,030
So deboss cloud is a serverless hosting platform for transact applications.

208
00:14:39,030 --> 00:14:43,751
So say so you can run your transact applications anywhere on your own laptop. or on a kubernetes cluster.

209
00:14:43,760 --> 00:14:45,590
But if you don't have any resources.

210
00:14:45,590 --> 00:14:51,269
and if you don't want to manage any clusters you can deploy your app to deboss cloud.

211
00:14:51,279 --> 00:14:57,350
So if I do use deboss transact library I must get a Postgress can I.

212
00:14:57,350 --> 00:15:02,310
if I wanted to use another database system could I currently deboss transact supports.

213
00:15:02,310 --> 00:15:10,389
any Postgress compatible databases so you can use it with your own Postgress server like many of our.

214
00:15:10,389 --> 00:15:12,389
users just simply add deboss transact.

215
00:15:12,389 --> 00:15:15,110
because they already have a Postgress server.

216
00:15:15,110 --> 00:15:20,710
so they just use the same server to store your execution state data.

217
00:15:20,710 --> 00:15:25,910
but you can also use other offerings like each cloud provider has some Postgress offering.

218
00:15:25,910 --> 00:15:32,230
and we also compatible with new serverless Postgress like neon.

219
00:15:32,230 --> 00:15:36,629
or superbase or cockroach db or yugabet db there are a lot of options here.

220
00:15:36,629 --> 00:15:41,350
so is that just because that's the database you tested with.

221
00:15:41,350 --> 00:15:45,590
or is there any specific features of postgress postcress compatible databases.

222
00:15:45,590 --> 00:15:47,509
that you leverage yeah I guess it goes.

223
00:15:47,509 --> 00:15:50,069
back to the question of why do we choose postgress.

224
00:15:50,079 --> 00:15:54,710
So like there are a couple of reasons we chose Postgress.

225
00:15:54,710 --> 00:15:57,670
First is that the ecosystem is huge.

226
00:15:57,670 --> 00:16:00,629
As I said there are a lot of providers in the cloud.

227
00:16:00,629 --> 00:16:02,949
or there are a lot of on-prem solutions as well.

228
00:16:02,959 --> 00:16:05,189
People know how to operate Postgress.

229
00:16:05,199 --> 00:16:11,671
It's a very mature and battle tested technology and people really trust Postgress. So that's one reason.

230
00:16:11,671 --> 00:16:15,350
And the second reason is that it's a relational database.

231
00:16:15,350 --> 00:16:18,311
So it has built-in transactions. It's very reliable.

