00:23:18,870 --> 00:23:20,390
So I think the workflow definition in deboss.

332
00:23:20,390 --> 00:23:27,430
and temporal are essentially the same but the core difference is how we perceive the implementation of this.

333
00:23:27,430 --> 00:23:31,190
durable execution.

334
00:23:31,190 --> 00:23:33,430
So in temporal we call the pattern external orchestration pattern.

335
00:23:33,440 --> 00:23:37,990
With external orchestration which means you have to start a temporal server.

336
00:23:37,990 --> 00:23:40,070
and then when you run your workflow.

337
00:23:40,070 --> 00:23:48,550
instead of directly executing each step your workflow function will need to incue a step into a temporal. worker.

338
00:23:48,550 --> 00:23:50,950
and then temporal worker temporal server will.

339
00:23:50,960 --> 00:23:59,191
instead push the notification or will instead notify a worker node to process that. They call it activity.

340
00:23:59,191 --> 00:24:02,149
We call it a step.

341
00:24:02,149 --> 00:24:04,230
And then after the worker node finishes a step or activity,

342
00:24:04,230 --> 00:24:05,990
they will send it back to temporal server.

343
00:24:05,990 --> 00:24:13,909
and temporal server will persist the result and then send it back to the main workflow function.

344
00:24:13,909 --> 00:24:19,669
So basically for every invocation of your step there are multiple network hops.

345
00:24:19,669 --> 00:24:26,230
and then by contrast in deboss we essentially embed dup execution in your program.

346
00:24:26,230 --> 00:24:29,750
So when you call the workflow it's just a function call.

347
00:24:29,750 --> 00:24:32,070
and when a workflow calls a step it's again it's a function call.

348
00:24:32,080 --> 00:24:36,549
but a function call is intercept by the deboss library.

349
00:24:36,549 --> 00:24:38,870
So everything will happen just in your program.

350
00:24:38,870 --> 00:24:43,990
So we believe the benefit of deboss is that it's really simple.

351
00:24:43,990 --> 00:24:46,549
All you need is your program and your postgress database.

352
00:24:46,549 --> 00:24:50,390
You don't have to deploy an extra orchestration server.

353
00:24:50,390 --> 00:24:55,669
and you don't have to deploy like distributed workers to process your steps.

354
00:24:55,669 --> 00:25:00,710
I get the part about not having to deploy an orchestration server.

355
00:25:00,710 --> 00:25:04,310
but then how do you achieve orchestration?

356
00:25:04,310 --> 00:25:07,990
So deboss implements job execution this as orchestration.

357
00:25:07,990 --> 00:25:15,110
So just to clarify that we achieve job execution by intercepting the function calls.

358
00:25:15,110 --> 00:25:17,190
So you can think of it as.

359
00:25:17,190 --> 00:25:19,269
when you call a deboss decorated function.

360
00:25:19,269 --> 00:25:27,750
instead of directly executing the function we wrap around the workflow function for example to say.

361
00:25:27,750 --> 00:25:30,070
we first persist the input and then call the function.

362
00:25:30,070 --> 00:25:34,870
When execute the workflow function the workflow function will call each step.

363
00:25:34,870 --> 00:25:38,230
and again each step is also wrapped by deboss.

364
00:25:38,230 --> 00:25:41,269
So in the wrapper deboss will first check.

365
00:25:41,269 --> 00:25:46,870
if the step has finished before if not execute it persist the result.

366
00:25:46,870 --> 00:25:49,350
if it has been executed before directly return.

367
00:25:49,350 --> 00:25:53,909
a result so everything happens in the language layer.

368
00:25:53,909 --> 00:25:59,270
so that's why we don't need to send it over to a separate worker to a separate message. cue to achieve.

369
00:25:59,270 --> 00:26:05,350
this is there any other areas where it's important to compare.

370
00:26:05,350 --> 00:26:12,310
and contrast with temporal I think The two main area one is simplicity like in order to add.

371
00:26:12,320 --> 00:26:16,390
deboss to your program it's really simple you install it.

372
00:26:16,390 --> 00:26:20,789
as a library add to your existing program by decorating your functions and you're done.

373
00:26:20,789 --> 00:26:26,149
and then to implement something temporal you have to.

374
00:26:26,149 --> 00:26:32,071
basically restructure program into like you have to think in a distributed system way every time. when you cut out.

375
00:26:32,071 --> 00:26:37,190
a step it is essentially an RPC call to another worker.

376
00:26:37,190 --> 00:26:42,390
and then to executed and and then wait for the result to come back.

377
00:26:42,390 --> 00:26:48,070
So we think simplicity is the number one differentiator and then it's also very simple to operate.

378
00:26:48,070 --> 00:26:53,350
So say like to run deboss in production all you need is a Postgress server.

379
00:26:53,350 --> 00:26:57,430
and then basically people already know how to operate Postgress servers.

380
00:26:57,430 --> 00:26:59,830
So we don't add much of the operational overhead to it.

381
00:26:59,830 --> 00:27:04,710
By contrast, if you use any like external orchestration services,

382
00:27:04,710 --> 00:27:08,950
you have to host their service or you have to rely on their cloud providers cloud offerings.

383
00:27:08,960 --> 00:27:16,951
For example, every time you want to call a step, you have to communicate to their cloud. and say, I want to execute a step.

