00:34:23,599 --> 00:34:28,790
Yeah, so actually I like to talk a bit more about workflow recovery.

490
00:34:28,790 --> 00:34:32,230
and failure recovery when you use in production.

491
00:34:32,230 --> 00:34:40,470
So as we talked earlier, it's true that developers can write checkpointing code manually.

492
00:34:40,470 --> 00:34:43,589
but you want to use a job execution system.

493
00:34:43,589 --> 00:34:49,909
or a library like deboss or others because you want to have automatic recovery.

494
00:34:49,909 --> 00:34:56,389
So give you a concrete example in production you usually you have to deploy your code in multiple.

495
00:34:56,389 --> 00:34:58,950
if you use Kubernetes you will deploy to multiple containers or pods.

496
00:34:58,960 --> 00:35:03,589
and in a large production development you could probably develop maybe a 100.

497
00:35:03,599 --> 00:35:06,390
or a thousand pods that each will serve.

498
00:35:06,400 --> 00:35:10,950
well you will load balance between those pods to serve requests.

499
00:35:10,950 --> 00:35:13,750
So the problem is that at any point in time.

500
00:35:13,750 --> 00:35:17,109
or within any hour some pods will definitely fail.

501
00:35:17,119 --> 00:35:21,829
So it's a probability to fail is high when you have a large deployment.

502
00:35:21,829 --> 00:35:28,150
And if you already use Kubernetes, you may think yes, I can just restart those pods.

503
00:35:28,150 --> 00:35:30,230
But the problem is that sure when you restart a pod,

504
00:35:30,230 --> 00:35:37,911
you still need some application logic to decide how to resume the work you've done before before you. crash.

505
00:35:37,911 --> 00:35:40,150
And if you use deboss, we provide a service called conductor.

506
00:35:40,160 --> 00:35:45,829
So this service basically connects to your running applications.

507
00:35:45,829 --> 00:35:50,550
and then it will detect when some of the workers are failing.

508
00:35:50,550 --> 00:35:53,109
So if it detects some workers failed,

509
00:35:53,109 --> 00:35:57,910
it will try to redistribute the pending workflows running on that worker to other healthy workers.

510
00:35:57,910 --> 00:36:00,870
And this way we automatically recover from failures.

511
00:36:00,880 --> 00:36:05,109
and we guarantee that all workflows will eventually run to completion.

512
00:36:05,109 --> 00:36:08,710
So I think this is one reason.

513
00:36:08,710 --> 00:36:15,910
that you really want to delegate this kind kind of recovery to some library or services like deboss.

514
00:36:15,920 --> 00:36:21,030
How does that coexist with the recovery mechanisms in Kubernetes?

515
00:36:21,040 --> 00:36:23,270
Yeah.

516
00:36:23,270 --> 00:36:27,349
So in Kubernetes basically when you restart it will restart your application.

517
00:36:27,349 --> 00:36:29,910
but it doesn't have any application semantics.

518
00:36:29,910 --> 00:36:31,829
It doesn't know what workflows failed before.

519
00:36:31,839 --> 00:36:38,470
Essentially deboss as a library will have some background threads to listen for tasks say you have to.

520
00:36:38,470 --> 00:36:40,550
recover this and that.

521
00:36:40,550 --> 00:36:45,430
So deboss will dispatch certain recovery commands to your workers.

522
00:36:45,430 --> 00:36:51,589
and this works perfectly with other mechanisms in like say kubernetes or other deployments.

523
00:36:51,589 --> 00:36:56,870
So what you mean is that I develop my application with the deboss transact library.

524
00:36:56,870 --> 00:37:02,950
and then when I deploy it to production I can leverage deboss conductor whether.

525
00:37:02,950 --> 00:37:05,349
or not I'm deploying to kubernetes.

526
00:37:05,349 --> 00:37:08,069
Yes, exactly.

527
00:37:08,069 --> 00:37:10,790
Do you use deboss conductor automatically in the deboss cloud?

528
00:37:10,800 --> 00:37:14,630
Yes, we have the same capability in deboss cloud.

529
00:37:14,630 --> 00:37:19,430
So if you deploy to deboss cloud you have all the automatic recovery plus autoscaling.

530
00:37:19,430 --> 00:37:22,790
All right, let's spend some time on AI agents.

