00:42:10,471 --> 00:42:15,349
So some of our use cases would be to scrape the website of stock stock markets.

595
00:42:15,349 --> 00:42:18,230
and then put into LM to some do some analysis.

596
00:42:18,230 --> 00:42:23,109
and then do some business decision based on like do I want to invest in the stock?

597
00:42:23,119 --> 00:42:26,550
Do I want to sell the stocks as a final step?

598
00:42:26,560 --> 00:42:28,390
Why durable execution?

599
00:42:28,400 --> 00:42:33,430
Yeah, durable execution is essential because you don't want to lose data.

600
00:42:33,430 --> 00:42:39,030
and once you proc like usually there are two reasons.

601
00:42:39,030 --> 00:42:41,829
One, the data volume is huge, right?

602
00:42:41,829 --> 00:42:46,710
So easily you can process 1,000 documents and if something fell in the middle.

603
00:42:46,710 --> 00:42:48,309
or not just 1,000 maybe 10,000 documents.

604
00:42:48,309 --> 00:42:52,150
if something fell in the middle you don't want to restart from the beginning like you don't want.

605
00:42:52,150 --> 00:42:54,950
to say oh now something.

606
00:42:54,950 --> 00:42:58,630
happened to my pipeline either the AI failed.

607
00:42:58,630 --> 00:43:00,390
or I I hit some AI rate limit.

608
00:43:00,390 --> 00:43:03,589
I don't want to say I have to reprocess all 10,000 documents again.

609
00:43:03,599 --> 00:43:06,470
I want to resume from where it left off.

610
00:43:06,480 --> 00:43:08,230
So that's the first thing.

611
00:43:08,230 --> 00:43:14,069
The second thing is you want everything to complete.

612
00:43:14,069 --> 00:43:18,309
Say if I process 10,000 documents, I want all of them to complete.

613
00:43:18,309 --> 00:43:22,630
Otherwise, my business decision for example, which stock to trade may be based on incomplete data.

614
00:43:22,640 --> 00:43:28,069
that can cause like financial loss and other sequence consequences.

615
00:43:28,079 --> 00:43:35,109
So in this specific example, if I did decorate the loading step with deboss.

616
00:43:35,119 --> 00:43:45,350
and the error was on let's say the 100,000 document in my set of 500,000 what is the. behavior?

617
00:43:45,350 --> 00:43:50,790
So basically in this case you want to use deboss cues to paralyze those tasks like you don't.

618
00:43:50,790 --> 00:43:54,950
want to process those all those documents in one giant step.

619
00:43:54,950 --> 00:43:59,589
You want to make sure that you want to.

620
00:43:59,589 --> 00:44:06,870
basically say each step is a cued task spawned by my workflow like from documents 1 to 10,000.

621
00:44:06,870 --> 00:44:10,151
I will incue. a task.

622
00:44:10,151 --> 00:44:15,109
and then it will become a fan out pattern and then we will wait until all tasks finish.

623
00:44:15,109 --> 00:44:17,910
So I will fan in by waiting those results.

624
00:44:17,910 --> 00:44:20,630
So if any of the tasks failed.

625
00:44:20,630 --> 00:44:23,589
then we'll when we recover the workflow we'll just say.

626
00:44:23,589 --> 00:44:27,270
okay now I have to check all those cute tasks.

627
00:44:27,280 --> 00:44:31,750
and if some tasks failed then we'll restart execution of those tasks.

628
00:44:31,750 --> 00:44:37,911
but for those tasks that have already finished we'll just get the result from the database. and then return it.

629
00:44:37,911 --> 00:44:42,870
So that's how you can correctly recover from those failures.

630
00:44:42,870 --> 00:44:49,670
So you had earlier mentioned about using conductor for deboss in production.

631
00:44:49,670 --> 00:44:53,670
Besides that, are there other recommendations about being able to run deboss at scale?

632
00:44:53,680 --> 00:44:56,390
Yeah.

633
00:44:56,390 --> 00:44:59,910
So the question is mostly about like the best practices for using deboss.

634
00:44:59,920 --> 00:45:02,871
Mhm. Yeah.

635
00:45:02,871 --> 00:45:04,630
So I think to run deboss at scale,

636
00:45:04,630 --> 00:45:08,710
you first really want to make sure that your workflows are deterministic.

637
00:45:08,710 --> 00:45:11,109
That's the core to guarantee your workflows are recoverable.

638
00:45:11,119 --> 00:45:14,950
because if you're you have nondeterminism in your workflow.

639
00:45:14,950 --> 00:45:19,990
when we recover it we may go through another execution path.

640
00:45:19,990 --> 00:45:23,430
that will we don't really know how to recover.

641
00:45:23,430 --> 00:45:27,030
And then the second thing is use cues wisely.

642
00:45:27,030 --> 00:45:32,870
So for example when you deal with LLMs they typically have rate limiting.

643
00:45:32,870 --> 00:45:34,470
and they also have concurrency limits.

644
00:45:34,470 --> 00:45:38,550
So how many outstanding requests you can have to those LLMs.

645
00:45:38,550 --> 00:45:40,950
So in this case use deboss cues.

646
00:45:40,950 --> 00:45:44,550
So with cues you can add ray limiter.

647
00:45:44,550 --> 00:45:48,790
Say I want at most five API calls uh within like 30 seconds.

648
00:45:48,790 --> 00:45:54,870
and I want at most 10 outstanding requests at a time.

649
00:45:54,870 --> 00:45:57,990
So with those primitives you can build your apps with parallel task.

650
00:45:57,990 --> 00:46:01,750
but also within the limit of those LLMs.

651
00:46:01,750 --> 00:46:06,230
Is there anything else you'd like to add for folks.

