00:48:58,470 --> 00:49:03,430
and by reliable I mean like yes those codes may be buggy.

692
00:49:03,430 --> 00:49:05,829
so You want to have a way to say if I caught a bug,

693
00:49:05,829 --> 00:49:08,069
I want to be able to investigate a bug.

694
00:49:08,069 --> 00:49:13,511
I want to be able to say restart my workflow from a specific step. and then fix the bug.

695
00:49:13,511 --> 00:49:17,910
So those are the capabilities where those durable execution engines really shine.

696
00:49:17,920 --> 00:49:27,030
because with those AI generated unreliable code like you want a way to correctly store those information for.

697
00:49:27,040 --> 00:49:29,750
debugging.

698
00:49:29,750 --> 00:49:31,589
and because I think the one advantage of deboss is.

699
00:49:31,589 --> 00:49:34,630
that because we store those like what step has executed.

700
00:49:34,640 --> 00:49:39,910
and at when the time stamp of it in a relational database.

701
00:49:39,910 --> 00:49:44,549
So we we really have this structured data for AI to analyze.

702
00:49:44,549 --> 00:49:47,990
It's easy for human to analyze and it will.

703
00:49:47,990 --> 00:49:50,309
also be easy for AI to analyze what's going wrong.

704
00:49:50,309 --> 00:49:53,109
So I think it's a really exciting new capability.

705
00:49:53,119 --> 00:50:01,589
that we are exploring and I think simplicity and structured data logging are important.

706
00:50:01,589 --> 00:50:04,150
So simplicity is that we actually try to add a prompt for deboss.

707
00:50:04,160 --> 00:50:09,990
and we give the prompt that will incorporate our latest version of the code.

708
00:50:09,990 --> 00:50:13,990
We'll basically give AI instructions on how to add deboss to your code.

709
00:50:13,990 --> 00:50:16,790
So with that we can just tell AI make this code durable.

710
00:50:16,790 --> 00:50:18,710
and then AI will correctly do that in one shot.

